<ProjectLayout
  slug="neural-pde-stability"
  description="Adapting von Neumann stability analysis to cell-average neural network PDE solvers, which conserve mass for any weights but are not stable by construction."
  authors="Akshat Valse, with Jue Yan and Mengyuan Yang"
  affiliation="Department of Mathematics, Iowa State University"
  abstract="A cell-average neural network solver advances a PDE solution one step at a time with a learned flux, and because the same network is evaluated at both faces of a cell, it conserves mass exactly, whatever its weights. Stability is not guaranteed by construction: networks trained identically up to the random seed, with training losses you cannot tell apart, can behave completely differently under long rollouts. The project asks whether you can tell, from a trained network alone and before committing to an expensive simulation, whether its rollout will hold together. We are adapting von Neumann stability analysis to trained solvers of this kind, toward stability conditions on the trained weights. The results are held back until the paper is submitted."
  buttons={[{ label: 'Write-up', href: WRITEUP, icon: 'github' }]}
  info="Manuscript in preparation · NSF D4 NRT · May 2026 – present"
>
  <!-- Introduction -->
  <Container first>
    <h1>Introduction</h1>
    <p class="text">
      A neural network can be trained to advance a PDE solution one step at a time, and it can be
      built so that it never creates or destroys mass, whatever its weights are. What that
      construction does not give you is stability.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Networks trained identically up to the random seed, with training losses you cannot tell
      apart, can behave completely differently over long rollouts: some track the reference
      solution, others develop oscillations that grow until the output is worthless (Figure 1).
      The project asks whether you can tell which one you have before paying for the simulation.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      We are adapting von Neumann stability analysis, the classical Fourier-mode test for
      finite-difference schemes, to these trained solvers, toward stability conditions on the
      trained weights.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      <em>This page sets out the problem and what we are doing about it. The results are held
      back until the paper, in preparation with Prof. Jue Yan and Mengyuan Yang, is
      submitted.</em>
    </p>
  </Container>

