    </svg>
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 1.</b> The problem, as a schematic rather than data. Networks trained identically up
      to the random seed reach training losses you cannot tell apart, yet can behave completely
      differently under long rollouts. The question at the bottom paraphrases the one the project
      asks.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- The setting -->
  <Container>
    <h1>The setting</h1>
    <p class="text">
      A conservative finite-volume scheme advances cell averages by differencing a numerical flux
      across cell interfaces. In a cell-average-based neural network solver that flux is learned
      rather than designed: a small network is trained to reproduce the time-averaged flux at an
      interface. One step of the solver reads
    </p>
    <Tex block src={EQ1} />
    <p class="text">
      where <Tex src={String.raw`\bar v_j^{\,n}`} /> is the average of the solution over cell
      <Tex src="j" /> at time step <Tex src="n" />, <Tex src={String.raw`\Delta t`} /> and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\Delta x`} /> are the time step and the cell width,
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathcal N(\cdot\,;\Theta)`} /> is the flux network with weights
      <Tex src={String.raw`\Theta`} />, and <Tex src={String.raw`V_{j\pm1/2}`} /> is its input at
      the face <Tex src={String.raw`x_{j\pm1/2}`} />. The public write-up does not say which cell
      averages make up <Tex src={String.raw`V_{j+1/2}`} />, so Figures 2 and 3 leave the network's
      input unspecified, and the toy network in Figure 4 uses a placeholder input.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Figure 2 puts the learned flux in the slot that a designed flux would fill. The designed
      flux on the right is one textbook example, first-order upwind for linear advection, shown
      only for comparison: it does not stand for the project's equation or reference scheme,
      neither of which is public.
    </p>
  </Container>

