    </svg>
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 3.</b> Why conservation comes for free, drawn for a periodic row of five cells with
      illustrative bar heights; <Tex src={String.raw`\mathcal N_{j+1/2}`} /> is short for
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathcal N(V_{j+1/2};\Theta)`} />. The network is evaluated once per
      face, and that one value enters the updates of the two cells that share the face, with
      opposite signs. Under each face the two terms cancel, and on a periodic grid the two end
      terms belong to the same face. Nothing here depends on training.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container>
    <p class="text">
      The argument never uses the values of the weights, so it holds for a network with random,
      untrained weights just as well. Figure 4 checks this live, in floating point, where the
      identity can only hold up to rounding: it computes the drift from an exact sum of the cell
      averages, rounded once at the end, and compares it with a rigorous bound on the rounding
      committed by the updates so far.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <Container width="extra-large" tone="gray">
    <PdeConservation />
    <p class="caption">
      <b>Figure 4.</b> A live check of identity (2) on a periodic grid, starting from a square pulse
      and a smooth bump on a constant background, with
      <Tex src={String.raw`\lambda = \Delta t/\Delta x`} />. The face flux is either a designed
      one, the textbook upwind flux <Tex src={String.raw`a\,\bar v_j`} /> at
      <Tex src={String.raw`x_{j+1/2}`} /> with <Tex src="a = 1" />, or a tanh network of width 8
      whose weights are random and untrained, drawn from the seed shown. In exact arithmetic the
      drift is zero for either flux and every seed; whatever the profile does, the mass stays put
      up to rounding. The network is an illustration of "whatever the weights", not a trained
      solver: its architecture, activation and two-cell input are placeholders chosen for this
      illustration, not a description of the project's networks, and the rollout says nothing
      about which trained networks are stable.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- The problem -->
  <NoteRow>
    <h1>The problem</h1>
    <p class="text"><b>Conservation is guaranteed by construction. Stability is not.</b></p>
    <p class="text">
      Training minimizes a one-step prediction error against a short reference trajectory. Nothing
      in that objective says anything about what happens when the scheme is iterated for thousands
      of steps on states the network never saw during training. In practice, networks trained
      identically up to the random seed, reaching training losses you cannot tell apart, can behave
      completely differently under long rollouts. Some track the reference solution. Others
      develop oscillations that grow until the output is worthless.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      Since the losses are indistinguishable, the loss cannot tell you which network you have. A
      short validation rollout is not much better, because the instability may take far longer to
      show itself than anyone is willing to simulate. So you are left choosing among minima with no
      principled basis for the choice.
    </p>
    <div class="table-wrapper">
      <table>
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Question</th><th>Answer</th><th>Why</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Is mass conserved?</td><td><b>Yes</b>, exactly, for any weights</td><td>The update telescopes, identity (2)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Are long rollouts stable?</td><td><b>Not guaranteed</b></td><td>Stability is not built into the construction</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Does the training loss tell stable from unstable networks?</td><td><b>No</b></td><td>The losses are indistinguishable</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Is a short validation rollout a better test?</td><td><b>Not much better</b></td><td>The instability may take far longer to show itself than anyone is willing to simulate</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
    <Fragment slot="note">
      It is the autoregressive-surrogate failure mode, in the simplest setting where the
      questions of classical numerical analysis can be put to it.
    </Fragment>
  </NoteRow>

  <!-- The question -->
  <Container>
    <h1>The question</h1>
    <p class="text">
      <b>Can you tell, from a trained network alone and before committing to an expensive
      simulation, whether its rollout will hold together?</b>
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      A trained solver is still a numerical scheme. Its weights are frozen once training ends, so
      what you have in front of you is a fixed, deterministic update rule that happens to have been
      fitted rather than derived, and numerical analysis has a long history of asking exactly this
      question about update rules of that kind. Whether that history carries over to a learned
      flux, and what it says when it does, is what the project is about.
    </p>
    <h2>Why it matters</h2>
    <p class="text">
      Neural PDE solvers are usually assessed by running them: train, roll out, measure the error.
      That is expensive, and it only certifies the trajectories you actually ran. Anything you can
      check on the weights themselves is cheap by comparison and does not depend on having picked
      the right test case.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      <b>Bears on:</b> learned operators · neural surrogates · rollout stability.
    </p>
  </Container>

  <!-- What we are doing (CV level of detail, no further) -->
  <Container>
    <h1>What we are doing</h1>
    <p class="text">
      We are adapting von Neumann stability analysis to cell-average neural network solvers of
      the form (1): deriving the linearized amplification factor of the learned time-stepping
      scheme and characterizing how perturbations propagate through it, toward stability
      conditions on the trained weights analogous to those of classical finite-difference
      methods.
    </p>
    <p class="text">
      I reimplemented the group's solver in Python to enable systematic stability experiments
      across schemes and step sizes.
    </p>
    <h2>The classical test, for reference</h2>
    <p class="text">
      In its textbook form, von Neumann analysis applies to a linear scheme with constant
      coefficients on a periodic grid. One step carries a single Fourier mode
      <Tex src={String.raw`\bar v_j^{\,n} = \hat v^{\,n} e^{\mathrm{i} j\theta}`} />, with
      <Tex src={String.raw`\mathrm{i} = \sqrt{-1}`} /> and
      <Tex src={String.raw`\theta \in [-\pi, \pi]`} />, to a multiple of itself,
      <Tex src={String.raw`\hat v^{\,n+1} = G(\theta)\,\hat v^{\,n}`} />, and the scheme is
      stable in this sense when <Tex src={String.raw`|G(\theta)| \le 1`} /> for every
      <Tex src={String.raw`\theta`} /> (more generally, <Tex src={String.raw`|G(\theta)| \le 1 + C\Delta t`} />
      for a constant <Tex src="C" />). For the designed flux of Figure 2, first-order upwind with
      <Tex src={String.raw`\nu = a\,\Delta t/\Delta x`} />,
    </p>
    <Tex block src={EQ3} />
    <p class="text">
      so <Tex src={String.raw`|G(\theta)| \le 1`} /> for every <Tex src={String.raw`\theta`} />
      exactly when <Tex src={String.raw`0 \le \nu \le 1`} />. This is the textbook case, shown for
      comparison only. It is not the project's scheme or result, and this page does not say how the
      analysis carries over to a learned flux.
    </p>
  </Container>

